# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  SECTION 3 -- FILE SYSTEM  (directory layout, atomic writes, health tracking)
#
#  Folder structure
#  ================
#  ~/activity_monitor/
#    data/
#      logs/         rotating log files        (2 MB × 3 = 6 MB max)
#      snapshots/    gzip JSON ring             (configurable count)
#      cache/        ephemeral runtime files    (data.json, dashboard.html)
#      exports/      on-demand CSV + JSON
#    activity.db     SQLite WAL database
#    config.json     user-editable configuration
#
#  Crash safety
#  ============
#  Every file write goes through _atomic_write_*:
#    1. Create a uniquely-named sibling tmp file (PID + monotonic counter)
#    2. Write all content
#    3. os.replace() — atomic on POSIX; atomic on Windows since Python 3.3
#    4. If any step fails, delete the tmp and return False (never raise)
#  A crash between steps 2 and 3 leaves a stale .tmp file.
#  _cleanup_stale_tmps() is called at startup to remove them.
#
#  Stability
#  =========
#  - Every mkdir uses exist_ok=True; each directory is attempted independently
#  - A write-probe confirms writability before committing to a base dir
#  - StorageHealth tracks disk space and consecutive failures; callers can
#    gate writes on is_healthy() to avoid spamming a full disk
#  - Orphaned .tmp files from previous crashes are cleaned on startup
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

